-- Passage wg12-argument-writing-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('7f0cd155-6a0b-5ba4-a5c4-57de51c0cd9a', 'wg12-argument-writing-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g12-argument-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Measuring Heat at Orland Valley$q$, $q$(1) Orland Valley High cancels outdoor practices only when the air temperature reaches 95 degrees, a rule that ignores humidity, sunshine and wind. (2) The athletic department should instead base its decisions on a wet-bulb globe temperature reading, a measurement that combines all four conditions into a single number. (3) According to the state athletic trainers' association, an afternoon of 88 degrees with high humidity can place more strain on the body than a dry afternoon of 96 degrees. (4) A handheld meter costs about 300 dollars, less than the price of one set of new uniforms. (5) The football team won the regional title in 2019.

(6) Some coaches argue that a more complicated system would lead to frequent, unpredictable cancellations and cost teams valuable preparation time. (7) However, the wet-bulb system does not simply cancel practices; it sets levels that call for longer water breaks, lighter equipment or a move to the early morning before a session is called off. (8) Last fall, the Brennick Conference used this tiered system at nine schools and canceled fewer practices than it had under its old air-temperature rule, because the tiers let teams adjust rather than stop.

(9) The district says it cares about athletes. (10) Now it is time to show it.$q$, 'draft')
