insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('fd561ad4-6a2e-585c-b1ba-633844e72912', 'wg12-argument-writing-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g12-argument-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Air Sensors for Tilbury Bus Stops$q$, $q$(1) Thousands of Tilbury residents wait at bus stops beside heavy traffic each day, yet the city measures air quality at only one monitoring station, located in a park two miles from downtown. (2) The city should install low-cost air sensors at its twenty busiest bus stops and publish the readings on a public website. (3) A student project at Tilbury Technical College placed four borrowed sensors at downtown stops for a month and found rush-hour fine particle levels that were often twice those reported by the park station. (4) With readings from each stop, the transit department could see which routes expose riders to the worst air and target shelters, plantings or schedule changes there. (5) Sensors are cool gadgets.

(6) Environmental engineers note that low-cost sensors are less accurate than the city's official monitor, especially in humid weather. (7) This limitation is real, and the city should not use the sensors to issue official health warnings. (8) The sensors could be checked each season against the park station, a method the college students used to keep their readings within about ten percent of the official values. (9) Even approximate readings would reveal which stops are worst, and that comparison is exactly what the city needs.

(10) In conclusion, air pollution is bad.$q$, 'draft')
